I won't go back to 2008, no.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.577
- Yeah.

00:01:03.577 --> 00:01:04.623
(laughing)

00:01:04.623 --> 00:01:05.883
- No, kidding, kidding.
- That wasn't me.

00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:06.780
(laughing).

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.960
- You've got a highlight
called stuff I'm bad at.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:09.870
- Oh, yeah.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:10.740
- Which--
- There's a lot of that,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:11.840
but what's on that one.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.681
- Which shows you been
terrible at charades.

00:01:13.681 --> 00:01:15.500
- Oh, god, that was humiliating.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.301
- What's something else
that you're famously bad at?

00:01:18.301 --> 00:01:20.090
- Everything, everything
- That's... Stop that.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:23.900
- And also any like "Two in
the bush one in the bag."

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.433
Give me one... what is it like...

00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:26.266
- Like can't...

00:01:26.266 --> 00:01:27.470
You can take a horse to water
but you can't make a drink.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:28.347
Stuff like that?
- Yeah, I would say like,

00:01:28.347 --> 00:01:29.822
"You can take a horse to get milk

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:31.970
"but it won't drink it."

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.840
And people would be like,
"That's not a thing."

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:35.680
- I'm interested to know,

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.733
who's the most fascinating
character that you have played?

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.340
- I think in the last year
I got to play Infinity

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.100
and then right after that I played...

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.010
It's a character named
Madison in Zombieland 2,

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:54.780
and jumping from those two
is really fascinating for me.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.810
That was...

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.170
There couldn't be...

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.140
They couldn't be more polar opposite.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.070
Infinity is diminutive
and ill and isolated.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.820
A lot of nice people heard about my chemo

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.050
and they paid for me to go
to Disneyland with my Nana

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:12.171
and we got to skip all of the
lines and it was so much fun.

00:02:12.171 --> 00:02:13.340
(laughing)

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.091
Best day ever.

00:02:15.091 --> 00:02:17.270
And then I played
Madison who's essentially

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:21.800
a amalgamation of or a version
of Paris Hilton in 2009.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.443
With big long fake nails
and long blonde hair and

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.070
juice good tracks.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:31.220
So getting to get those two
very distinct different voices

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:34.030
and looks and perspectives that was

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:37.050
so so rewarding and fun.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:38.810
- I have a question to ask you about

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.210
your parents and Ed Sheeran's
music video, "Perfect."

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.560
- I thought you said my
parents in Ed Sheeran's video.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.706
I was like, "Did my parents...
(laughing)

00:02:45.706 --> 00:02:47.299
- They're here (laughing).

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:50.259
- I was like, "My parents in
an Ed Sheeran's music video?"

00:02:50.259 --> 00:02:51.559
- Ed Sheeran also is here.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.423
That video has over two
billion views on YouTube but--

00:02:56.423 --> 00:02:57.256
- Does it?

00:02:57.256 --> 00:02:58.089
- Yes.

00:02:58.089 --> 00:02:58.922
- Wow.
- Yes.

00:02:58.922 --> 00:03:00.350
Can you tell me how that happened?

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:01.183
How did you--

00:03:01.183 --> 00:03:03.687
- I don't know, he was
like, "Zoey, come to Austria

00:03:03.687 --> 00:03:05.510
"And film this music video."

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.810
And I said, "Sure Ed, that's do it."

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:11.170
And it was filmed in this
little ski town in Austria

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.030
called Hintertux because
he wanted to make it

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:14.970
a Christmas video,

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:16.057
even though there was a lyric that says,

00:03:16.057 --> 00:03:17.250
"Barefoot in the grass."

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:18.760
I'm like this doesn't...

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.730
Like barefoot in the grass and we're just

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.010
frolicking through the snowy woods.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.478
I was just like, "Okay, whatever."

00:03:26.478 --> 00:03:28.907
I've no explanation
other than he was like,

00:03:28.907 --> 00:03:31.190
"Will you come do this
music video in Austria?"

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:33.527
I was like, "Yeah, okay sure."

00:03:33.527 --> 00:03:36.140
- "The Politician" is a
show that has a lot of heart

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.000
but it's also hilarious.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.900
- We could make some money on this?

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:40.733
- Absolutely.

00:03:40.733 --> 00:03:41.566
Oh, absolutely.

00:03:42.906 --> 00:03:44.680
- Oh, well hot damn.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.060
- Were there an scenes
where you kept laughing

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.860
or had to stop yourself from laughing?

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:49.693
- There's a part of a scene

00:03:49.693 --> 00:03:50.680
that didn't make it into the show

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.750
because Ben and I could
not get our shit together

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:57.560
in any way, shape or form
and it was a very very...

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:02.150
It was a three word improv ad-lib

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:06.270
that Jessica Lange did about
the ingredients in her dish,

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:07.470
which was a bologna cup.

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:11.239
- It smells great.

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.670
- Well?

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.053
- Bologna cups.

00:04:15.053 --> 00:04:15.886
Yay.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:19.770
Velveeta cheese and bologna.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:22.530
That was all she said and
the way that she said it

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:23.900
with all of her Langeness

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.260
had Ben and I crying of laughter.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.037
The whole crew crying with laughter.

00:04:28.037 --> 00:04:31.910
- "The Politician" centers
obviously around rival

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.003
political campaigns.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.550
If you were running for
student body president,

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:41.373
what obscure thing would
you promise people?

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.433
- First period would be puppy period,

00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:48.574
and you would spend it with
all slew of cute ass dogs.

00:04:48.574 --> 00:04:49.407
(laughing)

00:04:49.407 --> 00:04:50.240
- That's actually really...

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:51.418
You'd win.

00:04:51.418 --> 00:04:52.251
That's so nice.

00:04:52.251 --> 00:04:53.200
- Yeah, right?

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.190
Everyday would start with a puppy.

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:56.900
- Do you have a secret talent?

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.430
- I have one and I show
it in every interview

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:03.050
so it's so boring but I'm
so bad at so many things

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:08.020
and I'm really only good
at one skill that is

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.133
useless.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.580
And it's making myself look
like I have a facelift.

00:05:13.856 --> 00:05:14.700
- I have seen this

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.070
but I really want to see it again, please.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:16.903
- Okay.

00:05:20.816 --> 00:05:23.066
(laughing)

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.030
- What's a job that you
should you could do if money

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.763
and skill were no option?

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:35.300
I told you there were answers that short.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:36.453
- Neuroscientist.

00:05:37.341 --> 00:05:38.660
Just because that sounds smart.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:39.730
- That sounds really cool, yeah.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.859
- And my job sounds the opposite of smart.

00:05:41.859 --> 00:05:42.692
- That's not true.

00:05:42.692 --> 00:05:43.525
- No.

00:05:43.525 --> 00:05:44.923
What about you, what would you do?

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.360
I told you you'd get excentral.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.440
- Yeah, I know, you're right.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.016
No you're right.

00:05:51.016 --> 00:05:51.849
You know what.

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:53.220
I weirdly wanted to be
a synchronized swimmer

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.270
when I was younger.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.620
- That's so cool.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:56.453
- Yeah.

00:05:56.453 --> 00:05:57.286
- Have you ever done it?

00:05:57.286 --> 00:06:00.250
- Well, I used to swim and
then I was just like lazy

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:01.138
and I was like, "Forget it."

00:06:01.138 --> 00:06:02.350
You know, it's like when you are trying

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:03.580
to be like a pro at stuff.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.280
That's long.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.540
So I left it but I still
kind of want to do that.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:07.970
- But they have like groups of...

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:12.440
You can go and do synchronized
swimmer like things.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:13.273
- Yeah, that's true.

00:06:13.273 --> 00:06:14.980
- There's one in LA, I know for sure.

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:17.260
I was looking into it
with a friend of mine.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:18.435
- Oh, okay.

00:06:18.435 --> 00:06:20.360
- Because I'd thought it'd be
a weird, fun hobby to have.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:21.290
- There you go.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:23.570
So if you were forced to do an ice breaker

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.470
in a room full of new people
and you're ought to tell

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:28.863
the group one interesting
fact about yourself,

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:31.440
what fact would you choose?

00:06:36.351 --> 00:06:38.413
- I hate revolving doors

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:43.570
and scallops and everything
else is pretty tolerable

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:45.410
or even good and exciting.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:46.974
- Why don't you like revolving doors?

00:06:46.974 --> 00:06:47.807
- They're going to get you.

00:06:47.807 --> 00:06:48.882
- Yeah, they actually are, though.

00:06:48.882 --> 00:06:50.000
Like they really are.
- They're going to get you.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.440
They're going to get you.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.160
That's what they do, they get you.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.540
- Okay, Zoey thank you so
much for speaking with me.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.170
- Thank you.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:58.003
- Have a great time.

00:06:58.003 --> 00:06:58.836
I love "The Politician" by the way.

00:06:58.836 --> 00:06:59.669
You're fantastic in it.

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:00.502
- Thank you.

00:07:00.502 --> 00:07:02.060
- And these were your Netflix IX.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.227
- Netflix IX.

